input: { [key: string]: any },
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Runs a Weave tool (a tool created from a Weave workflow) and returns run ids; poll them with weave_get_tool_run_output. A pasted Weave URL is enough; never open a browser or use browser automation for Weave. An app.weavy.ai/tool/<id> URL names a tool. An app.weavy.ai/flow/<id> URL, or a request to run "the workflow", names a workflow: say running workflows isn't supported here, offer to run its tool instead (same id), and call this only after the user agrees. Call weave_get_tool_inputs first to learn the inputs. A paid run is gated: a call without `acknowledgedCost` only quotes and spends nothing, returning `status: "inputs_required"` or `cost_confirmation_required` with instructions to follow. A free run starts at once, so make sure the user wants it before calling, and never promise a cost preview: say a paid run is quoted first and a free one starts right away. Always show the user the cost and get an explicit Approve/Cancel (a structured prompt, not free text) before echoing `acknowledgedCost` to run — every run, including reruns — and confirm any auto-filled inputs (values you did not set that the run will use). Include the response's `costDisclosure` string as its own sentence after the question, not folded into it, the first time you quote a cost in a conversation; omit it after that. For a media input, pass the whole asset object from weave_upload_asset or a run output; pass external media as its https URL directly (only upload a local file). If the user wants an input you did not get from weave_get_tool_inputs, do not omit it or fold it into another input like the prompt: re-fetch weave_get_tool_inputs (its inputs can change) and set it. The tool's creator runs the workflow's latest edits unless you pass an older `version`; other users usually run the tool's current version.

```ts
