Use this first when the user names a person (a name with a company or domain, a LinkedIn URL or an email) or a company and wants contact details, a profile or firmographics, such as the email of a founder at a given company. Use it, not people_company_search, whenever you have one of those identifiers. Give people (name with company or domain, LinkedIn URL, email or phone) or companies (domain, name or LinkedIn URL), up to 10 records per call. Each requested field runs a waterfall: Firecrawl tries the providers that can use your inputs one at a time, by listed price, and stops when the field is found. Without contact_fields a person gets a profile and a company gets firmographics; with contact_fields (work_email, personal_email, mobile) each person gets those values and, unless profile is false, the profile too. Ask for mobile only when a phone number is needed: it is the costliest field. A work email is never on a known free-mail domain or role mailbox, and is on the company domain when one is found there; otherwise an address at another employer may be returned with domain_match false and warning "employer_mismatch" in fields.work_email: treat it as unconfirmed. fields says for each answer which provider gave it, whether the email was verified and its domain matched. verified_only returns only verified emails. Each result is the provider's raw response, tagged with the provider. A provider that finds the value is charged its listed price; misses are free. max_credits caps the call; without it there is no cap. To call one specific provider, use alexandria_library_read instead. Example: {"contact_fields":["work_email"],"people":[{"name":"Jane Doe","domain":"example.com"}]}.

